			"Message": "feat(Maintain): Add Eliminate module to inline single-use Rust let bindings\n\nIntroduce a new `Eliminate` module under the Maintain component that analyses Rust source files and inlines `let` bindings used exactly once, are non-mutated, and are not captured by closures. This mirrors the TypeScript Eliminate project but targets Rust ASTs via `syn` and `prettyplease`.\n\nThe module provides:\n- `Source/Eliminate/mod.rs` — Module root with documentation and re-exports.\n- `Source/Eliminate/CLI.rs` — Clap-based command-line interface for batch processing (with dry-run, verbose, and glob options).\n- `Source/Eliminate/Constant.rs` — Default glob pattern, max iterations, and initialiser size limit.\n- `Source/Eliminate/Definition.rs` — `Options` and `Stats` data structures.\n- `Source/Eliminate/Error.rs` — Custom error type wrapping IO, parse, and glob errors.\n- `Source/Eliminate/Fn.rs` — Top-level entry point that parses CLI arguments and runs the pipeline.\n- `Source/Eliminate/Logger.rs` — Initialises `env_logger` with a coloured format.\n- `Source/Eliminate/Process.rs` — File discovery (walkdir + globset) and orchestration of the transform per file.\n- `Source/Eliminate/Transform/` — Core AST transformation pipeline:\n    - `Collect.rs` — Scans blocks for structurally eligible `let` bindings.\n    - `Count.rs` — Counts references in subsequent statements (with shadow and closure detection).\n    - `Inline.rs` — `VisitMut` implementation that iteratively eliminates single-use bindings and wraps compound replacements in parentheses when needed.\n    - `Safe.rs` — Safety predicate: rejects `unsafe { … }` and oversized initialisers.\n    - `mod.rs` — Pipeline entry point: parse → iterate elimination → pretty-print.\n\nThe module is integrated into `Library.rs` via a new `pub mod Eliminate` and a branch in `main()` for the `eliminate` subcommand. Integration tests in `tests/Eliminate.rs` cover 25+ scenarios: simple inlining, chained bindings, shadowing, closure capture, safety constraints, and idempotency.\n\nThis addition improves codebase conciseness by removing unnecessary intermediate variables while respecting safety, size thresholds, and semantic correctness.",
